From 17d25d8862071d4272313c513ce9d071d8a733a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 12:46:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/8] feat(plugins): load plugins through module:Class
 entrypoints

Every shipped manifest declared `python -m forgemesh_<name>` as its
entrypoint, but no such module exists, so nothing could load a plugin
from its manifest. The CI smoke script ignored the declared value and
guessed which file to import instead, so the broken entrypoints went
unnoticed.

Entrypoints now use one form, `<module>:<Class>`, where the module is
resolved inside the plugin package (the plugin directory must contain
__init__.py; workflow-reference gains a docstring-only one). PluginSpec
validates the format and the JSON Schema uses the same pattern.
forgemesh.plugin.loader.load_entrypoint loads the package under a
private module name derived from its path, imports the declared module
and accepts only a class defined inside the plugin package, so the
entrypoint itself cannot name objects outside the plugin (for example
os:system). Loading still runs plugin code in-process.

All ten manifests, the single-node example and the scaffold template
now declare real entrypoints. scripts/plugin_entrypoint_smoke.py loads
every plugin through its declared entrypoint, instantiates the class
when its constructor needs no host-provided dependencies, and fails on
a missing or broken entrypoint instead of skipping it.

Breaking: manifests using other entrypoint forms (module commands, bare
class names) are rejected by PluginSpec and by the schema.
